WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.970
How did a concert film shatter box office
records?

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:07.230
Here's the truth behind the deal Taylor Swift
made with AMC, and how it's paying out beaucoup

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.900
bucks for the pop star.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:15.179
"Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour" made a reported
$92 million domestically in its opening weekend.

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:19.289
At the time of this video, the movie's total
worldwide box office earning is more than

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:22.722
$178 million and still climbing.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:27.210
That is a tremendous amount of money for a
documentary, which is the film genre the concert

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.896
movie technically falls into.

00:00:28.896 --> 00:00:33.610
According to Out Magazine, the film broke
AMC's record for first-day pre-sales, grossing

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:39.230
$26 million in its first 24 hours of being
on sale, outperforming the previous record-breaker,

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:41.251
"Spider-Man: No Way Home."

00:00:41.251 --> 00:00:45.523
Now, since it's been out, it's already become
the highest grossing concert movie of all time.

00:00:45.523 --> 00:00:49.820
The previous king was Justin Bieber, whose
"Never Say Never" grossed over $99 million

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.700
at the global box office.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.410
This also makes "The Eras Tour" the first
concert movie to make more than $100 million.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.470
Why was it such a big hit?

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.190
Well, let's start with the obvious stuff.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:04.330
Taylor Swift is more popular than ever before
in her 17-year career.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.465
"I'm a Swiftie."

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:15.009
This certainly fueled the sensation that is
The Eras Tour, and the fervor for this tour

00:01:15.009 --> 00:01:17.610
has only made Swift's spotlight shine brighter.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.850
Swift has cemented herself as the biggest
act in pop music at this point in time.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.910
The tour showed just how large and diverse
Taylor Swift's fanbase is to sell as many

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.729
tickets as it has for the prices they've gone
for.

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:33.000
Her astronomical rise has even been on the
minds of many culture writers; The Guardian

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.640
credits Swift's current ubiquity partly to
the new pop music economy where radio play

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.040
matters less than cult-like followings discussing
their favorite artist's every move on Reddit

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.229
and TikTok.

00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:47.160
No matter the reason, the success of The Eras
Tour — both the tour and the concert movie

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.870
— happened because Swift has such an enduring
base of fans.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.280
This is the biggest factor in the success
of "The Eras Tour" film.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.729
Leftover demand from the North American leg
of the tour is insatiable; Taylor Swift found

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:03.600
a way to let even more money pour in while
giving fans a second chance to see the show

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.736
if they couldn't make it IRL.

00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:09.649
"I didn't get to go to the tour, so I'm excited
to see it on the big screen."

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:14.209
Of course, it took a sizable investment to
make the movie — $10 to $20 million according

00:02:14.209 --> 00:02:18.080
to Puck — but that has more than paid off
for Swift now that the film has taken control

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.300
of the box office.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.980
The tickets for The Eras Tour were so scarce
and the resale prices were so high that it

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.390
nearly broke the Internet.

00:02:26.463 --> 00:02:30.390
Even though this is Taylor Swift we're talking
about, we have to take into account the history

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.230
of the genre when talking about expectations.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.300
While concert films occasionally turn out
to be cinematic masterpieces like "Gimme Shelter"

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:42.459
or "Stop Making Sense," these aren't always
the biggest money makers in a given year.

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:46.830
Again, the highest-grossing concert movie
until now was Justin Bieber's "Never Say Never,"

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.099
which didn't crack $100 million.

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:53.300
In a film landscape dominated by big studios
and streaming platforms, it's been quite a

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.115
while since a concert movie has felt like
an event.

00:02:56.115 --> 00:03:00.230
Comparing this to the year's biggest movies
— for instance, "Barbie" made almost $1.5

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.060
billion — Swift's box office takeaway might
not seem like much.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.780
It's worth noting, though, that the budget
for a concert documentary is often 10 times

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.900
lower than that of a Hollywood production.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.349
Reportedly, the budget for "The Eras Tour"
was $20 million.

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:17.440
Compare that to, say, the $200 million budget
for "Killers of the Flower Moon."

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.840
"Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour" came to theaters
on October 13, 2023 — a week with no other

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.125
major new movies.

00:03:24.125 --> 00:03:28.099
All the biggest movies at the box office the
week "The Eras Tour" came out were released

00:03:28.099 --> 00:03:29.140
the week before.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.069
The movie that wound up being its biggest
competition, "The Exorcist: Believer," even

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:36.020
got out of the way in anticipation of Swift's
dominance.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.650
Blumhouse founder Jason Blum tweeted the decision,
saying:

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.080
"Look what you made me do.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.889
The Exorcist: Believer moves to 10/6/23. #TaylorWins."

00:03:44.889 --> 00:03:48.930
Even though it came out a week before "The
Eras Tour," "The Exorcist: Believer" struggled

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:52.540
anyway, making $26 million in its opening
weekend.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.790
Word of mouth on that specific sequel was
pretty sour, but even with a better critical

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.739
response, an R-rated horror movie was never
going to compete with the marketing force

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:02.390
that is Taylor Swift.

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.890
Outselling "Killers of the Flower Moon" in
the concert film's second week in theaters

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.629
is a much more impressive feat.

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:11.550
Yes, "Killers" is a three-and-a-half-hour
R-rated drama, but it's from "Goodfellas"

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:16.070
and "The Departed" director Martin Scorsese
and features the considerable star power of

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.620
Robert De Niro and Leonardo DiCaprio.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.180
"This wealth should come to us."

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:27.480
Yet, despite the mostly glowing reviews, "Killers
of the Flower Moon" could not compete with "The Eras Tour."

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.139
In a normal world, tickets to The Eras Tour
would have been affordable for every family

00:04:31.139 --> 00:04:33.090
to bring their kids if they wanted to.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.540
Still, even if you could afford tickets, other
factors got in the way: It's harder to travel

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.520
with children, and kids have a harder time
staying up late for a long concert.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.820
Eras Tour setlists were routinely over three
hours long.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.610
But if your kid falls asleep in the middle
of a three-hour movie, well, it's not as big of a deal.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.300
Plus, it's a lot easier to get everyone together
and file out of a movie theater than it is

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.979
a sold-out stadium.

00:04:54.979 --> 00:04:58.480
In the time it would take you to get to the
concert stadium parking lot, you can make

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.539
the drive home from the movie theater — or
stick around and watch it again.

00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:05.380
"I'm gonna go to this movie at least 13 times."

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:10.020
Young people are a large part of Taylor Swift's
demographic and the fans who were too young

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.910
to go to the show can see the movie with their
parents.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:16.830
Immediately, that makes for a bunch of tickets
sold, as evidenced by the pre-sale numbers

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.330
that "The Eras Tour" movie put up.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:23.370
There's a strong argument for the IMAX potential
of "Taylor Swift: The Eras Tour."

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.900
The appeal is obvious.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.169
You are already going to the movie not for
a narrative, but for the immersive experience

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:31.340
you can only get from being in a movie theater.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:36.606
IMAX's strengths enhance the audiovisual experience
of movies with an emphasis on spectacle.

00:05:36.606 --> 00:05:40.560
"The Eras Tour" is all spectacle and a natural
fit for the format.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:46.009
On opening weekend, the film was shown on
625 IMAX screens in 45 markets around the

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:51.690
world and IMAX sales accounted for a double-digit
percentage of the opening weekend pre-sales.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:56.059
That is a pretty massive percentage for a
format most movies don't even get released in.

00:05:56.059 --> 00:06:00.280
More screens mean more IMAX tickets sold,
and of course, theaters charge more for IMAX

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.610
tickets, which factors into the box office
success of "The Eras Tour."

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.821
One of the reasons "The Eras Tour" film got
made was that Taylor Swift took it on as a personal project.

00:06:09.821 --> 00:06:14.069
According to Puck, Swift and her family put
up the reported $20 million that it cost to

00:06:14.069 --> 00:06:15.389
fund the project.

00:06:15.389 --> 00:06:18.729
It's not a surprise that they would be able
to make this happen, given how much money

00:06:18.729 --> 00:06:21.580
they have made on The Eras Tour alone.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:26.050
According to Fortune, the tour is set to be
the first to ever shatter $1 billion made

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:31.099
in ticket sales — that's not even including
the $75 hoodies and $45 t-shirts.

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:34.650
When you're the biggest pop star in the world,
it turns out you don't need something as silly

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.050
as a movie studio to make something happen.

00:06:37.599 --> 00:06:38.502
"It's not not true."

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.430
When it comes to the current competition,
we've established that "The Eras Tour" is

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.660
blowing everyone out of the water.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.210
But let's put that success in comparison to
some of the biggest movies of the year.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:56.910
"Barbie" is the biggest hit of the year, grossing
over $1.4 billion at the worldwide box office.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:00.819
Its competitor-slash-companion "Oppenheimer"
fell just shy of $1 billion.

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:05.160
"The Eras Tour" was never going to hit the
heights of these giants, nor that of "The

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.419
Super Mario Bros. Movie," which grossed a
mighty $1.36 billion.

00:07:09.419 --> 00:07:13.759
If we look closer to where "The Eras Tour"
lands on the list, it seems sure to slip into

00:07:13.759 --> 00:07:15.307
the top 20 for the year.

00:07:15.307 --> 00:07:20.990
At $178 million in the first two weeks of
release, "Eras" is looking to overtake "Teenage

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:25.620
Mutant Ninja Turtles: Mutant Mayhem," "The
Equalizer 3," and "Insidious: The Red Door"

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.560
with another weekend of strong sales.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.419
The way the deal between Taylor Swift and
AMC Theaters shook out is another potential

00:07:32.419 --> 00:07:34.370
reason for its runaway success.

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:39.794
AMC agreed to a contract with Swift that earned
her 57% of the earnings on the film and left

00:07:39.794 --> 00:07:41.365
AMC the rest.

00:07:41.365 --> 00:07:45.650
According to Variety, this is a smaller cut
than many major studios take — Marvel is

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:50.879
cited as an example of a studio that receives
closer to 65% of the box office profits.

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:55.270
Getting an advantageous deal could be quite
a boon for AMC as it turns out the company

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.020
has a hit on its hands.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:01.400
"They announced Taylor Swift's movie broke
the record for the most pre-sale tickets sold

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.889
in one single day, and their CEO is predicting
this will be very lucrative for the company."

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:11.710
According to MarketWatch, shares of AMC have
fallen 73.3% in 2023, although the comparatively

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:16.050
successful summer haul of "Barbie" and "Oppenheimer"
created some cause for optimism.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:20.379
Taylor Swift's concert movie lighting up the
box office may just continue the upward trend

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:22.669
for the movie theater company.

00:08:22.669 --> 00:08:27.610
There are a couple of key things to glean
from the box office success of Taylor Swift's "The Eras Tour."

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:31.940
For starters, Swift's partnership with AMC
sets a precedent for concert films going forward

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:36.075
— one that has already been followed up
by the other biggest female pop act in the world.

00:08:36.075 --> 00:08:40.650
Beyonce's upcoming ambitious concert film
"Renaissance" is going to be distributed through

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.894
AMC Theaters in a similar fashion.

00:08:42.894 --> 00:08:47.310
According to Variety, Beyonce negotiated directly
with AMC for the release of the filmed version

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:48.990
of her Renaissance World Tour.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:52.320
"Did the girls of summer save the US economy?"

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.130
This doesn't just feel like the way of the
future for concert movies — a genre that

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:59.899
has been increasingly relegated to streaming
platforms as the years go on.

00:08:59.899 --> 00:09:04.310
In the wake of recent labor tumult in the
land of Hollywood, AMC is opening the possibility

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:10.040
of working directly with filmmakers and creatives
without the need for a studio to act as a distributor.

